[0.000, "o", "n 3.13 release add for free-threading</div> <div class=\"doc-meta\">LLMFlow-Search Research Report • Generated 20 September 2026, 19:43 • ID: res_0d792cb1a333</div> <hr class=\"top-rule\"/> <p><em>Evidence window: no source carries a publication date · 2 sources · 2 undated · compiled 2026-09-20</em></p> <h1>Free-threading in Python'\r\r\n"]
[0.119, "o", "\r\r\n# Free-threading in Python 3.13: what the official sources document\r\r\n\r\r\nThis covers the free-threading additions in the Python 3.13 release, as described by the Python project's own documentation (the What's New in Python 3.13 page and the free-threading HOWTO). The evidence is a bounded selection — the official documentation pages — not an exhaustive archive of every commit or PEP.\r\r\n\r\r\n## What was added\r\r\n\r\r\nThe What's New in Python 3.13 page reports that Python 3.13 was released on October 7, 2024, and its release highlights list \"experimental support for running in a free-threaded mode (PEP 703)\" as one of the biggest changes, alongside a new interactive interpreter and a JIT compiler [3].\r\r\n\r\r\nThe core addition: the What's New in Python 3.13 page reports that CPython now has experimental support for running in a free-threaded mode with the global interpreter lock (GIL) disabled [3]. Free-threaded execution allows full utilization of available processing power by running threads in parallel on ava"]
[0.000, "o", "ilable CP"]
[0.000, "o", "U cores; programs designed with threading in mind will run faster on multi-core hardware, though not all software benefits automatically [1][3].\r\r\n\r\r\nThe free-threaded build is a separate build, not a replacement for the default one. The What's New in Python 3.13 page reports that it requires a different executable, usually called `python3.13t` or `python3.13t.exe` [3]. The What's New in Python 3.13 page reports that pre-built binaries marked *free-threaded* can be installed as part of the official Windows and macOS installers, or CPython can be built from source with the `--disable-gil` configure option [1][3].\r\r\n\r\r\n## Experimental status\r\r\n\r\r\nThe What's New in Python 3.13 page states the feature \"is an experimental feature and therefore is not enabled by default,\" and that \"the free-threaded mode is experimental and work is ongoing to improve it: expect some bugs and a substantial single-threaded performance hit\" [3]. The free-threading HOWTO likewise describes it as a build of Python \"called free threading"]
[0.000, "o", " where"]
[0.000, "o", " the global interpreter lock (GIL) is disabled\" [1].\r\r\n\r\r\nThe experimental status is also visible in the interpreter's own version string: the What's New in Python 3.13 page reports that `python -VV` and `sys.version` contain \"experimental free-threading build\" [3]. (The HOWTO, which documents later versions, describes the string as \"free-threading build\" [1]; the 3.13 What's New wording is the one that applies to this release.)\r\r\n\r\r\n## How the GIL is controlled at runtime\r\r\n\r\r\nThe free-threading HOWTO reports that free-threaded builds support optionally running with the GIL enabled at runtime, using the `PYTHON_GIL` environment variable or the command-line option `-X gil` [1]. The What's New in Python 3.13 page gives the specific form `-X gil=1` for re-enabling the GIL [3].\r\r\n\r\r\nThe free-threading HOWTO reports that the GIL may also be automatically enabled when importing a C-API extension module that is not explicitly marked as supporting free threading, and a warning is printed in that case [1]. The What's"]
[0.000, "o", " New in "]
[0.000, "o", "Python 3.13 page reports that importing C extensions that don't use the marking mechanisms causes the GIL to be enabled, unless the GIL was explicitly disabled with `PYTHON_GIL` or `-X gil=0` [3].\r\r\n\r\r\n## New APIs and configuration variables for detecting free-threading\r\r\n\r\r\n| Item | What it does | Source |\r\r\n|---|---|---|\r\r\n| `sys._is_gil_enabled()` | New function to check whether the GIL is actually disabled in the running process | [1][3] |\r\r\n| `sys.version` / `python -VV` | Contain \"experimental free-threading build\" (3.13 wording) | [3] |\r\r\n| `sysconfig.get_config_var(\"Py_GIL_DISABLED\")` | Returns `1` if the build supports free threading; described as the recommended mechanism for build-configuration decisions | [1] |\r\r\n| `Py_mod_gil` slot | C API slot used to indicate that an extension module supports running with the GIL disabled | [3] |\r\r\n| `PyUnstable_Module_SetGIL()` | For extensions using single-phase init, indicates whether they support running with the GIL disabled | [3] |\r\r\n| `PyMutex` | New lig"]
[0.000, "o", "htweight mu"]
[0.000, "o", "tex occupying a single byte | [3] |\r\r\n\r\r\nThe What's New in Python 3.13 page also notes that pip 24.1 or newer is required to install packages with C extensions in the free-threaded build [3].\r\r\n\r\r\n## Note on scope\r\r\n\r\r\nThe sources describe the free-threaded build's thread-safety behavior, known limitations (immortalization, frame objects, iterators, single-threaded overhead, memory usage) and behavioral changes (context variables, warning filters) in the free-threading HOWTO [1], but that page documents the current documentation set rather than the 3.13 release specifically — for example, it states \"As of the 3.14 release, immortalization is limited to...\" [1].\r\r\n\r\r\n\u001b[38;2;112;143;181m\u001b[1mSources:\u001b[0m\r\r\n  [1] Python support for free threading — Python 3.14.7 documentation — https://docs.python.org/3/howto/free-threading-python.html\r\r\n  [2] Get started with the free-threaded build of Python 3.13 | InfoWorld — https://www.infoworld.com/article/3552750/get-started-with-the-free-threaded-build-of-python-3"]
[0.000, "o", "-13.html\r\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  [3] What’s New In Python 3.13 — Python 3.14.7 documentation — https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.13.html\r\r\n\r\r\n\u001b[38;2;112;143;181m\u001b[1mCoverage note (not fully covered by sources):\u001b[0m\r\r\n  - note: Identifies experimental free-threaded build and PEP 703\r\r\n  - note: Describes what free-threading enables and changes\r\r\n  - note: Notes experimental status and non-default enablement\r\r\n  - note: Cites official What's New and HOWTO sources\r\r\n\u001b[38;2;112;143;181m\u001b[1mPDF report:\u001b[0m reports/2026-09-20_what-did-the-python-3-13-release-add-for-free-threading_res_0d792cb1a333.pdf\r\r\n\u001b[38;2;120;129;145m\u001b[2m──────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\r\r\n  Steps: 7 | Type next question or 'exit'\r\r\n"]
